<h2>One seed, two products</h2>
<p>Guar gum starts as the same raw material regardless of end use — the seed of the guar plant (<em>Cyamopsis tetragonoloba</em>), grown predominantly in Rajasthan, Haryana and Gujarat. What happens after harvest is where the two products diverge, and the divergence is significant enough that a buyer sourcing one grade shouldn't assume a quote for the other is comparable in any way.</p>

<h2>Food grade</h2>
<p>Food-grade guar gum goes through refining specifically to meet food-safety purity standards, removing contaminants and controlling for consistency in a way that supports its use as a thickener, stabiliser or texturising agent in food products — everything from dairy alternatives to sauces to gluten-free baking. It's specified by viscosity (within food-application-relevant ranges), mesh size for powder fineness, and purity, and typically ships with documentation confirming it meets the food-safety standards applicable to the destination market.</p>

<h2>Industrial grade</h2>
<p>Industrial-grade guar gum is processed and tuned for viscosity performance in technical applications — most prominently oil and gas drilling fluids (including hydraulic fracturing), but also paper manufacturing, textiles and mining. It doesn't carry food-safety certification and isn't processed to the same purity standards, because its performance requirements are entirely different: viscosity behaviour under specific pressure and temperature conditions matters more than food-safety purity for this use case.</p>

<h2>Why this distinction matters for pricing</h2>
<p>Two things follow from this split market. First, comparing a food-grade quote to an industrial-grade quote is comparing different products — don't do it. Second, because both grades draw on the same underlying guar seed crop, a surge in industrial demand (often tied to oil and gas drilling activity, which moves with energy prices) can tighten overall seed supply and affect pricing across both grades simultaneously, even though the downstream markets have nothing to do with each other. If you're a food-grade buyer and see an unexpected price move, industrial demand cycles are a common, non-obvious cause.</p>

<h2>How to specify your order correctly</h2>
<ul>
  <li>State your end use explicitly — food application or technical/industrial application.</li>
  <li>For food grade, specify viscosity range, mesh size, and confirm the food-safety documentation your market requires.</li>
  <li>For industrial grade, specify the viscosity performance your application needs — this is typically a technical specification your engineering or procurement team will already have.</li>
</ul>
<p>Get this right at the enquiry stage and the rest of the quoting process moves quickly, since it determines everything else about how we source and specify your order.</p>
